## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates your understanding of end-to-end performance testing for complex streaming architectures under bursty conditions, including backpressure behavior and cross-region autoscaling.

## Key Concepts
- End-to-end latency distribution and tail latency (p99, p999) in multi-stage pipelines
- Backpressure propagation and saturation points across Kafka, Flink, feature store, and inference services
- Regional autoscaling, circuit breakers, and traffic shaping strategies
- Realistic workload modeling with appropriate traffic patterns and mix

## Code Example
```java
// Example: Backpressure simulation using Kafka Streams
public class BackpressureTest {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.put("application.id", "latency-test");
        props.put("num.stream.threads", 4);

        // Configure max poll records to control backpressure
        props.put("max.poll.records", 100);
        props.put("max.poll.interval.ms", 300000);

        // Set producer buffering for burst handling
        props.put("buffer.memory", 67108864);
        props.put("batch.size", 16384);

        KafkaStreams streams = new KafkaStreams(builder.build(), props);
        streams.start();

        // Monitor backpressure metrics
        streams.metrics().forEach((name, metric) -> {
            if (name.contains("poll-latency")) {
                System.out.println(name + ": " + metric.metricValue());
            }
        });
    }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical performance planning skills for ML inference services with batching, autoscaling, and cache effects in real-world production deployments.

## Key Concepts
- Tail latency under batching and its impact on P99/P999 percentiles
- Batch window vs. throughput trade-offs: larger windows increase throughput but add latency
- Observability: metrics, traces, and dashboards for end-to-end visibility
- Autoscaling triggers: latency thresholds, queue depth, and custom metrics
- Failure modes: GPU memory pressure, OOM kills, batcher stalls, and throttling

## Code Example
```javascript
import http from 'k6/http';
import { check, sleep } from 'k6';

export const options = {
  stages: [
    { duration: '60s', target: 100 },   // Warm-up
    { duration: '120s', target: 500 },  // 5x burst
  ],
  thresholds: {
    http_req_duration: ['p(99)<500'],    // P99 < 500ms
    http_reqs: ['rate>1000'],            // >1000 RPS
  },
};

export default function () {
  const res = http.post('https://inference.service/predict', 
    JSON.stringify({ input: 'data' }),
    { headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' } }
  );
  check(res, { 'status is 200': (r) => r.status === 200 });
  sleep(Math.random() * 0.1);  // Random inter-arrival
}
```

## Workload Model
| Parameter | Cold Cache | Warm Cache |
|-----------|------------|------------|
| Request Rate | 100-500 RPS | 100-500 RPS |
| Burst Factor | 3x-5x | 3x-5x |
| Batch Window | 10-100ms | 10-100ms |

## Acceptance Criteria
- P99 latency < 500ms under 5x burst with warm cache
- Autoscaling responds within 60 seconds of traffic spike
- Zero failed requests during burst (error rate < 0.1%)
- Throughput scales linearly with batch size up to GPU memory limit

## Why This Is Asked

Tests must cover tail latency under bursty demand, backpressure across services, and capacity planning for a high-variance data path like vector indices. This question probes practical workload modeling, observability, and fail-open/fail-closed behavior in real microservice architectures.

## Key Concepts

- Workload modeling with ramp, steady-state, and data variance
- Tail latency targets (p95/p99) under microservice cascades
- Backpressure mechanisms: bounded queues, bulkheads, flow control
- Circuit breakers and graceful degradation across gateway, auth, recommender, cache, and index
- Observability: tracing, metrics, logs, and correlation IDs

## Code Example

```javascript
